{
  "dom_id": "step-q21045",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
